1
00:00:03,930 --> 00:00:06,360
Completely empty box.

2
00:00:06,360 --> 00:00:07,670
If you'd like to examine it...

3
00:00:07,670 --> 00:00:08,990
Mm-hmm. Yep.

4
00:00:08,990 --> 00:00:12,240
I see nothing in this box but a wasted childhood.

5
00:00:13,820 --> 00:00:16,260
Little snarky there, cello Lessons.

6
00:00:17,740 --> 00:00:21,890
And we have this completely ordinary cylinder.

7
00:00:21,890 --> 00:00:23,350
If you'd like to examine it...

8
00:00:24,100 --> 00:00:25,520
Ordinary, yet I sense it is

9
00:00:25,520 --> 00:00:27,670
dripping with magical potential.

10
00:00:29,180 --> 00:00:31,040
Oh, dear Lord.

11
00:00:31,040 --> 00:00:34,850
A man pops out for a moment to evacuate his bowels

12
00:00:34,850 --> 00:00:37,410
and catch up on the adventures of the Caped Crusader,

13
00:00:37,410 --> 00:00:39,370
only to emerge and discover his apartment

14
00:00:39,370 --> 00:00:42,240
has been transformed into a cabaret.

15
00:00:43,570 --> 00:00:44,910
Sheldon, he's just practicing for

16
00:00:44,910 --> 00:00:46,150
his cousin's birthday party.

17
00:00:46,150 --> 00:00:48,020
As I was saying...

18
00:00:48,020 --> 00:00:49,990
empty box, empty cylinder,

19
00:00:49,990 --> 00:00:52,020
and... ooh!

20
00:00:52,020 --> 00:00:53,660
Voila.

21
00:00:54,230 --> 00:00:55,930
I'm telling you, dude, there's a seat on

22
00:00:55,930 --> 00:00:58,470
the Hogwarts Express with your name on it.

23
00:00:59,650 --> 00:01:01,080
This is how you're going to entertain

24
00:01:01,080 --> 00:01:02,600
your little cousin and his friends?

25
00:01:02,600 --> 00:01:03,950
By lying to them?

26
00:01:03,950 --> 00:01:05,750
How is this lying?

27
00:01:05,750 --> 00:01:08,570
A magic show is an inherently deceitful proposition.

28
00:01:08,570 --> 00:01:10,850
"This is an ordinary top hat."

29
00:01:10,850 --> 00:01:13,050
"You've chosen that card freely."

30
00:01:13,050 --> 00:01:16,680
"I do not have a set of lock picks lodged in my keister."

31
00:01:17,780 --> 00:01:19,490
Can't you just enjoy the wonder, Sheldon?

32
00:01:19,490 --> 00:01:21,180
Why must you peek behind the curtain?

33
00:01:21,180 --> 00:01:22,810
Or up the butt?

34
00:01:23,570 --> 00:01:26,820
If we poison the critical thinking faculties of children

35
00:01:26,820 --> 00:01:29,210
by telling them that rabbits come out of hats,

36
00:01:29,210 --> 00:01:33,570
then we create adults who believe in astrology and homeopathy

37
00:01:33,570 --> 00:01:35,620
and that Ryan Reynolds was a better choice

38
00:01:35,620 --> 00:01:38,520
for Green Lantern than lovable rogue Nathan Fillion.

39
00:01:40,500 --> 00:01:41,200
Sheldon,

40
00:01:41,200 --> 00:01:43,400
he's just gonna do a few magic tricks for some kids.

41
00:01:43,400 --> 00:01:44,730
I really don't think they're gonna end up

42
00:01:44,730 --> 00:01:46,480
liking the Green Lantern movie.

43
00:01:47,260 --> 00:01:48,460
Don't be so hard on him.

44
00:01:48,460 --> 00:01:51,570
It's natural to be a little cranky when you have...

45
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:53,910
a quarter in your ear!

46
00:01:56,260 --> 00:01:59,250
Coins lodged in body parts is not a source of amusement.

47
00:01:59,250 --> 00:02:00,970
When I was five, Billy Sparks put

48
00:02:00,970 --> 00:02:03,150
a Mexican peso up my nose.

49
00:02:05,510 --> 00:02:06,830
Wait-wait-wait, how is that not amusing?

50
00:02:06,830 --> 00:02:08,770
It's still there.

51
00:02:10,690 --> 00:02:13,900
Takes me 45 minutes to get through airport security.

52
00:02:17,050 --> 00:02:19,670
Look, I made Sheldon disappear. Ta-da.

53
00:02:20,760 --> 00:02:22,840
Next time, you should open with that.

54
00:02:48,530 --> 00:02:50,390
Ooh, look who's out on a date.

55
00:02:50,390 --> 00:02:53,270
Pasadena's favorite power couple, Shamy.

56
00:02:54,300 --> 00:02:56,360
And that is the answer to the question,

57
00:02:56,360 --> 00:02:58,930
what is wrong with eating at The Cheesecake Factory?

58
00:02:59,530 --> 00:03:01,810
So, are we celebrating anything special tonight?

59
00:03:01,810 --> 00:03:03,010
Oh, yes.

60
00:03:03,010 --> 00:03:04,920
Our relationship agreement specifies

61
00:03:04,920 --> 00:03:06,640
that the second Thursday of every month,

62
00:03:06,640 --> 00:03:09,190
or the third Thursday in a month with five Thursdays,

63
00:03:09,190 --> 00:03:10,560
is date night.

64
00:03:14,310 --> 00:03:15,970
That is so hot.

65
00:03:18,670 --> 00:03:22,630
All right, without objection, the minutes of the previous date

66
00:03:22,630 --> 00:03:25,200
are considered read and agreed to.

67
00:03:25,670 --> 00:03:27,430
Any new business?

68
00:03:27,430 --> 00:03:28,780
How was your day?

69
00:03:28,780 --> 00:03:30,200
Superb.

70
00:03:30,200 --> 00:03:32,570
This morning I made a palindrome

71
00:03:32,570 --> 00:03:33,850
with my Alpha-Bits.

72
00:03:33,850 --> 00:03:37,100
"Nice hat, Bob Tahecin."

73
00:03:38,600 --> 00:03:40,650
Sounds like you hit the ground running.

74
00:03:41,340 --> 00:03:43,840
I have a bit of good news myself.

75
00:03:43,840 --> 00:03:45,230
My most recent paper

76
00:03:45,230 --> 00:03:47,160
on how a cooperative long-term potentiation

77
00:03:47,160 --> 00:03:49,270
can map memory sequences in dendritic branches

78
00:03:49,270 --> 00:03:51,490
made the cover of Neuron.

79
00:03:51,490 --> 00:03:53,850
Ooh! Speaking of good news,

80
00:03:53,850 --> 00:03:54,960
somebody...

81
00:03:54,960 --> 00:03:58,040
just hit 100 Twitter followers.

82
00:03:59,350 --> 00:04:00,300
That's nice.

83
00:04:00,300 --> 00:04:02,180
Anyway, I've been dreaming of this day

84
00:04:02,180 --> 00:04:03,780
- for a long time. - Yeah, me, too.

85
00:04:03,780 --> 00:04:05,510
Triple digits, I'm not gonna lie--

86
00:04:05,510 --> 00:04:06,860
it feels pretty good.

87
00:04:07,900 --> 00:04:08,510
Sheldon,

88
00:04:08,510 --> 00:04:11,020
I'm the sole author on a paper being published

89
00:04:11,020 --> 00:04:12,590
in a distinguished journal that may change

90
00:04:12,590 --> 00:04:14,030
the course of my field.

91
00:04:14,990 --> 00:04:17,270
Ooh, 101!

92
00:04:17,270 --> 00:04:19,400
Air's getting a bit thin up here.

93
00:04:21,340 --> 00:04:23,310
So, are we ready to order?

94
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:24,890
Give me a minute.

95
00:04:24,890 --> 00:04:26,720
I'm gonna go wash up.

96
00:04:28,100 --> 00:04:29,620
Well, that's odd.

97
00:04:29,620 --> 00:04:32,290
We both washed up when we came in.

98
00:04:32,290 --> 00:04:34,810
It's probably a euphemism for urination.

99
00:04:35,560 --> 00:04:37,830
Sheldon, what is wrong with you?

100
00:04:37,830 --> 00:04:40,460
Not much. Although, I can be faulted

101
00:04:40,460 --> 00:04:42,990
for being overly fond of koala bears.

102
00:04:42,990 --> 00:04:44,340
I don't know what it is,

103
00:04:44,340 --> 00:04:46,140
when they start munching on eucalyptus,

104
00:04:46,140 --> 00:04:47,920
I just melt inside.

105
00:04:48,760 --> 00:04:50,760
Okay, Amy just told you some exciting news,

106
00:04:50,760 --> 00:04:52,610
and you acted like it was no big deal.

107
00:04:52,610 --> 00:04:54,640
Oh, I see why you're confused.

108
00:04:54,640 --> 00:04:57,780
No, her news sounded important, but what you're forgetting is,

109
00:04:57,780 --> 00:05:00,630
it was an achievement in the field of biology.

110
00:05:00,630 --> 00:05:03,100
That's all about yucky, squishy things.

111
00:05:05,530 --> 00:05:07,560
Honey, she's upset. You're her boyfriend.

112
00:05:07,560 --> 00:05:09,140
You have to at least try to be excited

113
00:05:09,140 --> 00:05:11,400
by the things she's excited by.

114
00:05:11,900 --> 00:05:13,900
What if they simply don't excite me?

115
00:05:13,900 --> 00:05:16,910
Well, just smile and think about koalas.

116
00:05:17,510 --> 00:05:18,880
She'd see right through that.

117
00:05:18,880 --> 00:05:20,330
We go to the zoo all the time.

118
00:05:20,330 --> 00:05:22,370
She knows my koala face.

119
00:05:23,780 --> 00:05:25,900
And for future reference, it's this.

120
00:05:31,390 --> 00:05:33,470
Oh, you have a lot of magic stuff.

121
00:05:33,470 --> 00:05:34,180
Yeah.

122
00:05:34,180 --> 00:05:35,980
I started when I was a teenager.

123
00:05:35,980 --> 00:05:38,320
I thought I could show a girl a few tricks

124
00:05:38,320 --> 00:05:42,430
and invite her up to my bedroom to see the rest of the act.

125
00:05:43,510 --> 00:05:45,130
Did it work?

126
00:05:45,130 --> 00:05:46,740
Ah, let's just say the only wand

127
00:05:46,740 --> 00:05:49,220
that ever saw any action was this one.

128
00:05:52,640 --> 00:05:57,240
Oh, look what my mom made us for the act.

129
00:05:58,370 --> 00:05:59,770
I like the fabric.

130
00:05:59,770 --> 00:06:00,830
Where'd she get it?

131
00:06:00,830 --> 00:06:03,730
Well, she cut up one of her old bathing suits.

132
00:06:06,700 --> 00:06:10,440
She made these two vests and half a dozen napkins.

133
00:06:10,840 --> 00:06:14,060
Has she tried on the vest yet?!

134
00:06:14,060 --> 00:06:15,690
I just gave it to her!

135
00:06:15,690 --> 00:06:18,840
I hope it fits-- she has a tricky figure!

136
00:06:18,840 --> 00:06:21,840
She's short and stacked, like me!

137
00:06:23,370 --> 00:06:27,630
She's not stacked like you, Ma! She never steps on hers!

138
00:06:31,790 --> 00:06:33,820
Listen, Howie, maybe I'm not the best choice

139
00:06:33,820 --> 00:06:35,510
to be a magician's assistant.

140
00:06:35,510 --> 00:06:38,210
You'll do fine. Hand me those rings?

141
00:06:39,580 --> 00:06:41,290
See? You nailed it.

142
00:06:41,920 --> 00:06:44,860
You know, i-it's just that I'm not that comfortable with little kids.

143
00:06:44,860 --> 00:06:47,070
Well, that's because you haven't been around them much.

144
00:06:47,070 --> 00:06:48,980
This is good practice. I mean,

145
00:06:48,980 --> 00:06:52,560
you are gonna be a mom someday, right?

146
00:06:52,560 --> 00:06:54,460
Yeah, sure.

147
00:06:56,090 --> 00:06:59,800
I haven't seen this trick in years.

148
00:06:59,800 --> 00:07:01,470
It's called the dove pan.

149
00:07:01,470 --> 00:07:03,630
You let everyone see the pan is empty,

150
00:07:03,630 --> 00:07:07,390
but there's a secret compartment in the lid.

151
00:07:07,390 --> 00:07:10,430
And then you open it and produce a live...

152
00:07:20,960 --> 00:07:22,950
Don't look in there.

153
00:07:27,910 --> 00:07:30,550
Oh, a little Red Dead Redemption, huh?

154
00:07:31,180 --> 00:07:32,610
Yes.

155
00:07:32,610 --> 00:07:33,880
How come you're not doing a mission?

156
00:07:33,880 --> 00:07:35,800
You're just wandering around.

157
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:37,740
Had a rough night.

158
00:07:37,740 --> 00:07:40,470
I thought I'd go for a walk and clear my head.

159
00:07:43,320 --> 00:07:46,250
Some people go outside and do that.

160
00:07:46,250 --> 00:07:49,870
It's after 9:00-- at this hour the streets of Pasadena

161
00:07:49,870 --> 00:07:53,100
are teeming with drunken sailors and alley cats.

162
00:07:54,480 --> 00:07:56,180
You want to talk about it?

163
00:07:56,180 --> 00:07:57,210
No.

164
00:07:57,210 --> 00:07:58,800
I think I'll just go in this saloon

165
00:07:58,800 --> 00:08:00,860
and drink my troubles away.

166
00:08:06,470 --> 00:08:09,520
You know digital alcohol is never a solution.

167
00:08:11,420 --> 00:08:12,950
What's going on?

168
00:08:14,240 --> 00:08:16,440
Hard as this may be to believe,

169
00:08:16,440 --> 00:08:20,440
it's possible that I'm not boyfriend material.

170
00:08:23,940 --> 00:08:26,490
Glad I was sitting down for that.

171
00:08:27,660 --> 00:08:29,630
Did you and Amy get in a fight?

172
00:08:29,630 --> 00:08:33,210
Amy had a fight; I was being perfectly reasonable.

173
00:08:34,290 --> 00:08:35,210
I'm gonna have a whiskey.

174
00:08:35,210 --> 00:08:36,500
Do you want anything?

175
00:08:37,470 --> 00:08:38,370
No, I can't.

176
00:08:38,370 --> 00:08:40,600
I'm playing Grand Theft Auto later.

177
00:08:46,670 --> 00:08:48,630
Look, I'm no expert in women...

178
00:08:48,630 --> 00:08:50,120
I'll say.

179
00:08:51,200 --> 00:08:53,750
That's not necessary when someone's trying to help you.

180
00:08:53,750 --> 00:08:56,990
I'm sorry. It's the alcohol talking. Go on.

181
00:09:00,070 --> 00:09:04,110
Sometimes with women you want to listen to what upsets them

182
00:09:04,110 --> 00:09:08,790
and then show them that you can grow and change.

183
00:09:09,800 --> 00:09:12,380
Nuts to that. What else you got?

184
00:09:13,940 --> 00:09:15,570
I don't know what to tell you.

185
00:09:15,570 --> 00:09:16,960
Buy her something.

186
00:09:18,330 --> 00:09:20,410
How does that work?

187
00:09:20,960 --> 00:09:25,550
Well, you skip over any attempt to repair your emotional connection

188
00:09:25,550 --> 00:09:30,450
and you win back her affection with an empty financial gesture.

189
00:09:32,090 --> 00:09:35,990
Well, that approach has Sheldon Cooper written all over it.

190
00:09:37,240 --> 00:09:39,900
- Glad I could help. - It's appreciated.

191
00:09:39,900 --> 00:09:41,200
And if you ever manage to find a woman again,

192
00:09:41,200 --> 00:09:43,260
I'll be glad to return the favor.

193
00:09:46,440 --> 00:09:49,120
And now, all the rings are

194
00:09:49,120 --> 00:09:51,240
magically linked together.

195
00:09:54,640 --> 00:09:56,580
My fiance's wearing a magic ring, too.

196
00:09:56,580 --> 00:09:58,640
It made all my money disappear.

197
00:10:01,810 --> 00:10:02,960
Oh, ah, right.

198
00:10:07,650 --> 00:10:09,810
And now, the great Howdini's

199
00:10:09,840 --> 00:10:12,380
next miraculous illusion.

200
00:10:13,310 --> 00:10:15,600
When are we gonna have cake?

201
00:10:15,600 --> 00:10:19,070
After you've been thoroughly amazed.

202
00:10:20,570 --> 00:10:22,780
But we want cake now.

203
00:10:22,780 --> 00:10:26,560
Well, you're not getting cake right now, capisce?

204
00:10:27,890 --> 00:10:30,550
Okay. Okay.

205
00:10:31,960 --> 00:10:34,730
Now, my lovely assistant is going to bring me

206
00:10:34,730 --> 00:10:37,170
an ordinary pitcher of milk.

207
00:10:37,170 --> 00:10:38,580
I know how you do that trick.

208
00:10:38,580 --> 00:10:41,010
Do you know how to pipe down?

209
00:10:42,610 --> 00:10:44,790
Okay, we're going to

210
00:10:44,790 --> 00:10:47,410
roll up this newspaper.

211
00:10:47,410 --> 00:10:48,590
It's a fake pitcher.

212
00:10:48,590 --> 00:10:49,870
You got wax in your ears?

213
00:10:49,870 --> 00:10:52,780
The man said it's an ordinary pitcher.

214
00:10:54,220 --> 00:10:55,810
Howdini.

215
00:10:56,840 --> 00:11:01,270
Okay, we're going to stick this in here.

216
00:11:01,270 --> 00:11:04,870
And then I'm going to pour in the milk.

217
00:11:04,870 --> 00:11:05,530
I hope this works,

218
00:11:05,530 --> 00:11:07,770
because I didn't bring a change of pants.

219
00:11:08,850 --> 00:11:10,390
Look, I Googled it.

220
00:11:10,390 --> 00:11:12,370
It's a fake pitcher.

221
00:11:14,890 --> 00:11:15,830
That's it.

222
00:11:15,830 --> 00:11:17,050
No cake for you.

223
00:11:17,050 --> 00:11:19,570
Anyone else want to join the No Cake Club?

224
00:11:21,080 --> 00:11:22,800
She's just kidding, boys and girls.

225
00:11:22,800 --> 00:11:24,800
Everyone gets cake.

226
00:11:24,800 --> 00:11:26,450
Not him.

227
00:11:30,330 --> 00:11:32,800
Just give me the pitcher.

228
00:11:33,640 --> 00:11:35,190
Behold!

229
00:11:48,210 --> 00:11:49,780
Wrong pitcher.

230
00:11:55,650 --> 00:11:57,920
I told you I'm not good with kids.

231
00:11:57,920 --> 00:11:59,030
Yes, you did.

232
00:11:59,030 --> 00:12:01,730
Oh, I think my crotch is starting to curdle.

233
00:12:04,480 --> 00:12:06,790
The thing is, my mother worked full-time.

234
00:12:06,790 --> 00:12:09,480
I had to take care of my brothers and sisters.

235
00:12:09,930 --> 00:12:11,120
Yeah, so?

236
00:12:11,120 --> 00:12:12,740
Ugh, it was horrible!

237
00:12:12,740 --> 00:12:15,340
With their snotty noses and their poopy diapers

238
00:12:15,340 --> 00:12:17,780
and their little shrieky voices, always complaining.

239
00:12:17,970 --> 00:12:19,370
"I don't want to get dressed."

240
00:12:19,370 --> 00:12:21,210
"Joey keeps spitting in my mouth."

241
00:12:21,210 --> 00:12:23,200
"This isn't the way Mom makes waffles."

242
00:12:23,200 --> 00:12:25,180
Well, okay, put your hand in here.

243
00:12:25,180 --> 00:12:27,390
Let's see how you like this waffle!

244
00:12:29,340 --> 00:12:31,490
All right, settle down.

245
00:12:31,490 --> 00:12:32,330
Red light.

246
00:12:32,330 --> 00:12:33,800
Red light, red light!

247
00:12:34,060 --> 00:12:36,150
Okay, we're fine.

248
00:12:38,030 --> 00:12:38,680
I'm sorry.

249
00:12:38,680 --> 00:12:40,660
I know it makes me sound like a bad person,

250
00:12:40,660 --> 00:12:42,560
but I just don't like children.

251
00:12:42,560 --> 00:12:44,760
Yeah, no, we all got that.

252
00:12:47,260 --> 00:12:49,070
But don't you think it'll be different

253
00:12:49,070 --> 00:12:51,220
when the child is ours?

254
00:12:51,220 --> 00:12:53,930
Right, when it's our kid that's ruined my body

255
00:12:53,930 --> 00:12:56,920
and kept me up all night and I've got no career and no future

256
00:12:56,920 --> 00:12:58,940
and nothing to be happy about for the next 20 years,

257
00:12:58,940 --> 00:13:01,620
sure, that'll be completely different.

258
00:13:05,330 --> 00:13:06,860
Well, yeah.

259
00:13:12,600 --> 00:13:15,180
I don't think there's anything in this jewelry store

260
00:13:15,180 --> 00:13:16,630
that Amy would appreciate more

261
00:13:16,630 --> 00:13:19,090
than the humidifier we were just looking at at Sears.

262
00:13:21,030 --> 00:13:23,340
Oh, my God, now I know what I sound like to you

263
00:13:23,340 --> 00:13:24,990
when I say stupid stuff.

264
00:13:28,270 --> 00:13:30,410
Ooh, a pocket watch.

265
00:13:30,410 --> 00:13:32,160
Okay, I don't think Amy wants a pocket watch.

266
00:13:32,160 --> 00:13:35,410
No, but maybe she wants a man with a pocket watch.

267
00:13:36,810 --> 00:13:39,400
Still saying stupid stuff.

268
00:13:41,360 --> 00:13:42,560
Does she like bracelets?

269
00:13:42,740 --> 00:13:45,090
